  transaction, one [38;5;153mnotifications[39m row per subscriber except the actor. Reads are [38;5;153mWHERE user_id = ? AND read_at IS NULL[39m.
  Unread count is a [38;5;153mCOUNT[39m. Per-notification read state is a column, so "mark this one read" and "mark all read" are both
  trivial. Cost: write amplification — a task with 20 watchers writes 20 rows per edit — and someone who subscribes
  later sees no history. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mB. Event log + compute on read.[0m Only [38;5;153mtask_events[39m, plus a [38;5;153mlast_read_event_id[39m watermark on each subscription. Your
  unread list is a join: events on tasks you watch, newer than your watermark. No write amplification, one fewer table,
  and new subscribers get history for free. Cost: you get a watermark, not per-item read state — marking one
  notification read while leaving an older one unread is not expressible, and that's the interaction a bell menu is
  built around. The read query also grows with event volume. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mC. No event log; mutations insert notifications directly.[0m Fewest concepts, fewest tables. Cost: no activity history,
  and every mutation re-implements fan-out, so the rule "don't notify the actor" has to be remembered in five places
  instead of one. Adding a per-task activity feed later needs a backfill you can't do, because the history was never
  recorded. Complexity: trivial.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m The write amplification that argues for B is a scale problem you do not have — at demo scale it's a
  handful of rows per edit, and if it ever mattered the fix is batching, not a redesign. What you do have is a UI whose
  whole interaction model is per-item read state, which B structurally cannot give you. C is cheaper today and pays for
  it twice: no history, and fan-out logic smeared across every mutation. A keeps the event log as one append-only source
  of truth, which also means the future comments sub-project just adds an event type rather than a parallel pipeline.
